%N A401858 a(1) = 3; for n > 1, a(n) is the smallest positive integer not yet in the sequence that, together with some earlier term, forms two sides of a Pythagorean triangle.
%C A401858 Call two numbers partners if they are two sides of a Pythagorean triangle, so that a(n) is the least unused partner of an earlier term. The sequence is a permutation of the integers >= 3, because by a result of Ordower the partner graph on the integers >= 3 is connected.
%C A401858 The numbers n > 3 with exactly two partners, i.e., with A046081(n) = 1, are 4, the primes p == 3 (mod 4) with p >= 7, with partners (p^2-1)/2 and (p^2+1)/2, and the numbers 2*p for primes p == 3 (mod 4), with partners p^2-1 and p^2+1. Each such n occurs immediately after the first of its two partners to appear (proved in the Additional comments).
%C A401858 For a prime p >= 7 with p == 3 (mod 4), if p divides a(n), then max(a(1), ..., a(n)) > (sqrt(2)-1)*p^2. Call the maximal block of consecutive terms divisible by p and containing 3*p the tooth of p; teeth appear as vertical lines in the scatterplot.
%C A401858 For p = 1019 the tooth is a(301459..301773); divided by p, these 315 terms are a(1), ..., a(315) in their original order, except that 421, 29, 20, 12, 5 come first. The teeth in the first 10^8 terms are those of the 1078 primes p == 3 (mod 4) with 7 <= p <= 18743; in 382 of them the quotients from 3*p on keep the relative order they have in the sequence.
%C A401858 The least integer >= 3 not among the first 10^8 terms is the prime 17099, whose partners 146187900 and 146187901 both exceed max(a(1), ..., a(10^8)) = 145752135. Proofs, tooth statistics, the ratio a(n)/n at records, and first positions are in the Additional comments.
